---

**H3: The value that doesn't show up in Q1**

Strategic differentiation and organizational intelligence: better decisions, faster innovation, talent retention, proactive workforce development. How to make these legible to a board that wants a number.

---

**H2: The Leading Indicator Problem: Tracking Outcomes You Can't See Yet**

The mistake is measuring only lagging indicators (revenue impact, cost per decision) and ignoring leading indicators (model accuracy, adoption rate, time-to-insight). Six months in, the lagging indicators are empty — not because the program is failing, but because it's in the learning phase.

Section notes: Introduce the AI value scorecard concept. Walk through leading vs. lagging. Explain why the distinction matters for board reporting.

`[INSERT DIAGRAM: AI value scorecard showing leading vs. lagging indicators with example metrics in each column and approximate timeline for when each type of indicator becomes meaningful]`

---

**H3: What to measure in months one through three**

Leading indicators only. What "investing" looks like as a measurable state.

---

**H3: What to measure in months three through nine**

Learning phase indicators. When to worry and when to stay the course.

---

**H3: When compounding returns begin**

Month nine and beyond. What to show the board at the twelve-month mark.

---

**H2: Why Successful Pilots Stall at Scale**

Nearly 60 percent of enterprise AI pilots never make it to production. The failure almost never comes from the technology. It comes after the proof of concept, when the organizational infrastructure that was supposed to absorb the AI turns out not to exist.

Section notes: The pilot trap. Name the three failure modes: change management gaps, data governance gaps, undocumented business processes. This section should feel like a diagnosis, not a scolding.

---

**H3: The change management ratio most organizations get backwards**

One dollar of change management for every two dollars of technical build. Most organizations invert this. The result is technology that works and adoption that doesn't.

---

**H3: Data readiness as the real gating factor**

You can't fix data quality with a better model. Readiness is the single biggest predictor of program success. What readiness actually means before you invest in infrastructure.

---

**H2: The Board Conversation You're Not Having**

AI doesn't deliver ROI the way an ERP implementation does. An ERP goes live and you see process changes immediately. AI systems improve over time. If executives are expecting year-one financial returns, the expectation was set wrong — and that's a leadership problem, not a technology problem.

Section notes: The time-horizon reframe. What to say to a CFO who has spent two million dollars and sees nothing. How to set expectations without losing the budget.

---

**H3: The right question to ask before any AI program launches**

"What were you measuring before you started?" Establishing baselines as a prerequisite, not an afterthought.

---

**H2: The Talent Story Is Being Told Backwards**

The early narrative — AI displaces workers, headcount falls — is real in some roles. But the more consequential story is what happens to the people whose work gets augmented. Twenty to forty percent productivity improvement in knowledge worker roles. Employee satisfaction goes up, not down. The work that remains is the work people were hired to do.

Section notes: Reframe displacement as the smaller story. Lead with augmentation. AI's ability to surface workforce patterns enables proactive talent development. This is a genuine competitive advantage — not a talking point.

---

**H2: The Case Against Waiting**

The window to build durable AI capability is narrowing. Organizations that defer another two to three years will face structural disadvantage in talent attraction, customer retention, and innovation velocity. Early movers are not just ahead — they are building capability compounding that late movers cannot buy their way into quickly.

Section notes: Close with urgency, but evidence-based urgency. Avoid generic "act now" consulting pressure. Make the compounding advantage concrete.

